feat(pip): Add r__svp_lcd_flat to publish RT_SCREEN as RT_FLAT_SCREEN

The halo inside a screen type thermal sight is the pack's own glass lens model, and the shader says
so plainly:

    float3 normalmap = sample_lens_normalmap(I.tc0, 3);      // the display treated as a curved lens
    reflections.rgb  = sample_reflections(...) ...;          // environment reflection, ring shaped
    specular = sample_specular(...) ...;                     // the sun glint
    bool is_flat = RETICLE_TYPE == RT_FLAT_SCREEN;
    if (is_flat) specular = float3(0, 0, 0);
    if (!is_flat) reflections *= sample_vignette(tc);

RT_SCREEN (6) and RT_FLAT_SCREEN (8) are the two display types, and only 8 is exempt from that
lens model, so a screen type sight gets a spherical normal, an environment reflection and a sun
specular - whose locus is a ring, the halo the player sees. Two details of the optic make it
prominent: the t12 declares s3ds_reticle_type = 6, and its s3ds_dirt_intensity = 0.1 is low, while
the reflection weight is pow(1 - dirt, 10) - a low dirt value is exactly what lets the reflections
through at full strength. The echo1 nearby declares 8, so it should show no ring at all, which is a
prediction the player can check.

Engine side there is no way to switch those layers off - they are shader internal - so this adds
r__svp_lcd_flat (default 0): when set, the s3ds_param_3 binder publishes reticle type 6 as 8, the
branch the shader already has for a flat display. It is deliberately an experiment rather than a
default because the same value also selects the flat reticle projection branch (RETICLE_PROJECT 0),
so the reticle has to be looked at before that becomes the default.